---
// One proof card for the homepage "evidence from real use" section (issue #10).
//
// The card contract — the five fields issue #10 asks for, in this order:
//   1. the observed event or failure          -> "What happened"
//   2. the control or workflow change it drove -> "The control it forced"
//   3. the current result or status            -> "Where it stands"
//   4. an honest limitation or open item       -> "Still open"
//   5. a link to durable public evidence       -> the card footer links
//
// Sourcing rule for this file: every quantitative or efficacy claim below is
// traceable to the published case study at /case-studies/30-ai-assisted-commits/
// (canonical: https://shakacode.com/blog/audit-30-ai-assisted-commits/) or to a
// public issue/pull request linked inline. Nothing here is estimated,
// aggregated, or reconstructed from private work. The PR that added this
// section carries the claim-to-source table.
//
// Deliberately model-agnostic: the study could not attribute the incorrect
// security-advisory citation to any model, so this card makes no claim about
// which agent produces better or worse output.
const study = '/case-studies/30-ai-assisted-commits/';
const shakapacker = 'https://github.com/shakacode/shakapacker/pull/';
const failClosedIssue = 'https://github.com/shakacode/agent-workflows/issues/249';
---
<article class="card proof">
  <div class="proof-head">
    <span class="proof-tag mono">Case study · August 5, 2026</span>
    <h3>The AI reviewer found it. We merged anyway.</h3>
  </div>

  <dl class="proof-fields">
    <div class="proof-field">
      <dt class="proof-label mono">What happened</dt>
      <dd>
        Hours before merge, an AI reviewer flagged two real risks on one pull request: a transient
        <code>429</code> or <code>5xx</code> API failure could be treated like a confirmed delisting
        and delete a healthy provider row, and the final exhausted retry could still sleep up to 60
        seconds that no later attempt would use. Both findings sat inside a timeline of
        <strong>83 conversation items</strong>. The pull request merged with neither one answered or
        resolved. <strong>Detection succeeded. Integration discipline failed.</strong>
      </dd>
    </div>

    <div class="proof-field">
      <dt class="proof-label mono">The control it forced</dt>
      <dd>
        Current review findings have to be stateful. Before merge, each one gets exactly one
        disposition — fixed and verified, explicitly rejected or accepted with a recorded rationale,
        or converted into an owned follow-up with a defined trigger — and until then the merge gate
        fails closed. A green check means a process finished; it is not evidence that anything was
        dispositioned.
      </dd>
    </div>

    <div class="proof-field">
      <dt class="proof-label mono">Where it stands</dt>
      <dd>
        The adversarial audit of 30 recent
        <a href="https://github.com/shakacode/shakapacker">Shakapacker</a> commits that followed the
        incident found no critical or high-severity defects, and the four focused fixes it did
        produce are merged upstream: <a href={`${shakapacker}1240`}>fail closed when <code>PATH</code>
        is unset</a>, <a href={`${shakapacker}1241`}>make the Babel 8 smoke prove transformation</a>,
        <a href={`${shakapacker}1242`}>actually lint the merge-readiness helper</a>, and
        <a href={`${shakapacker}1239`}>correct stale upgrade references</a>.
      </dd>
    </div>

    <div class="proof-field">
      <dt class="proof-label mono">Still open</dt>
      <dd>
        The gate is not finished. <a href={failClosedIssue}>Fail closed on unsettled configured
        reviews</a> is still an open issue, so today the disposition rule is a practice a human has
        to hold, not something the system enforces. The study is also honest about a second limit:
        its worst artifact — an incorrect security-advisory citation in permanent commit history —
        could not be attributed to any model, because the change arrived from an outside pull
        request with no recorded provenance. Without model, reasoning level, workflow version, and
        gate configuration, “which agent produces more slop” is an anecdote, not a measurement.
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <div class="proof-links">
    <a class="proof-next" href={study}>Read the full case study →</a>
    <a class="proof-next proof-next-quiet" href="/case-studies/">All case studies →</a>
  </div>
</article>

<style>
  /* Reuses .card and the .mono/label idiom from global.css — same visual
     system as the adoption ladder above it, no parallel one. */
  .proof { display: grid; gap: 18px; }

  .proof-head { display: grid; gap: 6px; }
  /* --ink-dim, not --ink-faint: this tag carries the study's date and kind,
     so it clears 4.5:1 in both themes like every other label on the page. */
  .proof-tag {
    color: var(--ink-dim); font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .proof-head h3 { font-size: 1.18rem; margin-bottom: 0; }

  .proof-fields { display: grid; gap: 16px; margin: 0; }
  /* Two columns only where there is room for a readable measure beside the
     label; below that the label stacks above its answer. */
  .proof-field { display: grid; gap: 6px; }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .proof-field { grid-template-columns: 11rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 8px 24px; align-items: baseline; }
  }
  .proof-label { color: var(--ink-dim); font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .proof-field dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-dim); font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .proof-field dd strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .proof-field dd code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.88em;
    color: var(--ink); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 8%, transparent);
    border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
  }
  .proof-field dd a {
    color: var(--brand-ink);
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand) 40%, transparent);
    /* Long link labels wrap mid-word rather than pushing the card wide at 390px. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .proof-field dd a:hover { border-color: var(--brand-ink); }

  .proof-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px 22px; }
  .proof-next {
    color: var(--brand-ink); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.88rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand) 45%, transparent);
    padding-bottom: 1px; transition: border-color .18s;
  }
  .proof-next:hover { border-color: var(--brand-ink); }
  .proof-next-quiet { color: var(--ink-dim); border-bottom-color: var(--line-strong); }
  .proof-next-quiet:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--ink-dim); }
</style>
